%I A234517 #28 Aug 18 2026 22:22:07
%S A234517 2,4,6,12,24,36,60,72,120,180,240,360,420,480,720,840,1260,1680,2520,
%T A234517 5040,7560,10080,12600,15120,20160,27720,30240,32760,55440,65520,
%U A234517 83160,110880,131040,166320,196560,221760,277200,332640,360360,393120,415800,443520
%N A234517 Numbers k > 1 such that for all m >= k, e(m) <= e(k), where e(m) = log_m(sigma(m)), the exponent such that m^e(m) = sigma(m), and sigma is the sum of divisors function, A000203.
%C A234517 Old name: Sequence of numbers from A234515 such that A234515(n) > A234515(k) for all k < n.
%C A234517 A234515 = natural numbers n sorted by decreasing values of log_n(sigma(n)), where sigma(n) = A000203(n) = the sum of divisors of n.
%C A234517 Conjecture: the sequence a(n) for n >= 4 is not the same as the sequence of numbers from A234516 such that A234516(n) > A234516(k) for all k < n.
%C A234517 From _Peter Munn_, May 31 2026: (Start)
%C A234517 Abundance (A033880) specifies how to get from (twice) m to sigma(m) using addition; abundancy (A017665/A017666) gives the factor required to get from m to sigma(m) using multiplication; in contrast, this sequence uses a function, e(m) = log_m(sigma(m)), which gives the exponent to get from m to sigma(m) using exponentiation.
%C A234517 The records sequences for abundance and abundancy are A140522 and A004394 respectively. A similarly constructed record sequence would not be very useful here as the function log_m(sigma(m)) has its maximum value at m = 2. So we start here by listing 2 and continue likewise by listing k if the value of the function at k isn't surpassed by the value of the function at any larger number, m.
%C A234517 (End)
%e A234517 Table for m = 2..12, showing also sigma(m), the sum of the divisors of m, and e(m) = log_m(sigma(m)):
%e A234517    m      e(m)        sigma(m)
%e A234517    2 ^ 1.58496250... =   3
%e A234517    3 ^ 1.26185950... =   4
%e A234517    4 ^ 1.40367746... =   7
%e A234517    5 ^ 1.11328275... =   6
%e A234517    6 ^ 1.38685280... =  12
%e A234517    7 ^ 1.06862156... =   8
%e A234517    8 ^ 1.30229686... =  15
%e A234517    9 ^ 1.16735875... =  13
%e A234517   10 ^ 1.25527250... =  18
%e A234517   11 ^ 1.03628656... =  12
%e A234517   12 ^ 1.34097774... =  28
%e A234517 For m = 3, the table shows that e(3) = 1.26185950 is less than an exponent that appears later, for example, e(4) = 1.40367746. So 3 is not in the sequence.
%e A234517 Similarly for m = 5, e(5) < e(6) and for m = 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, each respective e(m) is less than e(12). So these integers, m, are not in the sequence.
%e A234517 For all m > 12, it can be shown that e(m) < e(12) = 1.34097774, so 12 is in the sequence; and given their respective e(m) in the table, 2, 4 and 6 can also be seen to satisfy the requirements to be in the sequence.
%o A234517 (PARI) lista(nn) = {v = vector(nn, n, if (n==1, 0, log(sigma(n))/log(n))); v = vecsort(v,,5); m = 0; for (n=1, #v, if (v[n] > m, m = v[n]; print1(m, ", ")););} \\ _Michel Marcus_, Dec 11 2014
%Y A234517 Cf. A002182 (numbers n such that tau(n) > tau(k) for all k < n).
%Y A234517 See the comments for the relationships with A004394, A033880, A017665, A017666, A140522, A234515, A234516.
%Y A234517 A004490 is a subsequence.
%Y A234517 Cf. A234518, A234519, A234520, A234521, A234522, A234523, A234524.
%K A234517 nonn
%O A234517 1,1
